# Every world is generated with a history laid over its regions, and the developer overlay paints
# the realms that hold them today.
#
# What there is to assert without pinning content (how many realms a world ends with is a property
# of the seed and of the generator's tuning, and belongs to --history-audit, not to a script):
#
#   - the history exists, with events, figures and realms, and a hash;
#   - the avatar stands in a region some realm holds or in unclaimed land, and the line says which;
#   - realms are listed with a government and a ruler;
#   - the K overlay is off until asked for, and it and the R overlay exclude each other;
#   - the history hash survives the save round trip (the history itself is regenerated, never saved).
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --silent --no-save

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 45

expect-no-state world-history (no world generated)
expect-state world-history events=
expect-state world-history hash=
expect-state world-history overlay=off
expect-state world-history avatar region=
expect-state world-realms government=
expect-state world-realms ruler=

history
history realms

# K paints the realms; R takes over with the regions and turns K off; R again leaves both off.
key K
expect-state world-history overlay=on
key R
expect-state world-regions overlay=on
expect-state world-history overlay=off
key R
expect-state world-regions overlay=off
expect-state world-history overlay=off

# The TRAVEL PLAN box names the destination's region and the realm holding it, from the history.
travel neighbour
wait
expect TRAVEL PLAN
expect Region
expect Realm

save roundtrip
quit
